\section{Discussion}
The results of Section~3 are unconditional statements about finite uniform probability
spaces, and those of Section~4 are exact computations on $S_3\times S_3$ and
$S_3\times_{C_2}S_3$. The arithmetic enters only through assumptions (A) and (B).
Coprime discriminants are only a proxy: what matters is that the resolvents differ. In
both cases the cross-talk equals $\log_2$ of the size of the common quotient of the two
Galois groups. Heuristics that combine several splitting conditions implicitly assume
zero cross-talk; the dichotomy gives an exact criterion for when this is justified and an
exact penalty when it is not.

\section{Future work}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Cross-talk equals the entropy of the common quotient}: is $I\ge\log_2|Q|$ for
a common quotient $Q$, with equality under balanced fibres?
\item \emph{$S_4$ and $S_5$ pairs}: exact values from the class table of
$S_4\times_{S_3}S_4$. Since the type sees only conjugacy classes, the value is expected
to lie below $\log_2 6$.
\item \emph{$k$-prime additivity}: $I=k\cdot I(T_1;T_2)$, with plug-in bias
$(3^k-1)^2/(2n\ln2)$.
\item \emph{Converse}: does $I=0$ force count independence and hence linear
disjointness at the level of Frobenius statistics?
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
Two $S_3$ dials read at the same prime are either exactly independent or share exactly
one bit. Which case holds is decided by whether their quadratic resolvents coincide. The
small positive numbers seen in experiments are sampling bias around an integer number of
bits fixed by Galois theory.

